ancer; management depends on prior radiation and may include dissection, APR removal, radiation (if not previously given), and/or chemotherapy.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>INGUINAL (GROIN) LYMPH NODES<|>place<|>Lymph nodes in the groin region; can be a site of recurrence and are monitored with repeated physical exams after recurrence treatment.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>CHEST<|>place<|>A body region imaged in surveillance (CT chest with or without contrast) to monitor for recurrence/spread.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>PELVIS<|>place<|>A body region imaged in surveillance and a reference region for metastasis definition (metastatic disease is beyond the pelvis).)##\n(\"entity\"<|>LIVER<|>place<|>A common distant organ to which anal cancer metastasizes; metastatic disease is treated with systemic chemotherapy as first-line therapy.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>LUNGS<|>place<|>A common distant organ to which anal cancer metastasizes; metastatic disease is treated with systemic chemotherapy as first-line therapy.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>KIDNEYS<|>place<|>Organs that can be damaged by platinum-based chemotherapy agents (eg, carboplatin, cisplatin, oxaliplatin); kidney function influences whether cisplatin can be used and may necessitate IV hydration.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>FINGERS<|>place<|>Body area that can develop neuropathy (numbness/tingling/cramping/pain) with extended oxaliplatin treatment.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>TOES<|>place<|>Body area that can develop neuropathy (numbness/tingling/cramping/pain) with extended oxaliplatin treatment.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>MOUTH<|>place<|>Site where chemotherapy can cause sores in the mouth lining (mucositis), listed among common regimen side effects.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>RECTUS ABDOMINIS<|>object<|>A lower-abdominal muscle frequently used (with skin, fat, and blood vessels) to create a muscle flap for reconstruction after surgery, improving perineal healing.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>STOMA<|>object<|>The surgically created opening on the surface of the abdomen formed by a colostomy; stool exits thr